<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand IV of Naples, Ferdinand III of Sicily</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> as Ferdinand IV from 1759. He twice fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> before French armies, in 1798 when the Parthenopean Republic was proclaimed and in 1806 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> gave Naples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a>. Restored in 1815, he united his realms in 1816 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies.jpg" alt="Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Raphael Mengs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mengs_-_Ferdinand_IV_of_Naples,_Royal_Palace_of_Madrid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 12 January 1751, the third son of King Charles VII and Queen Maria Amalia. When Charles succeeded his half-brother Ferdinand VI as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in August 1759, treaties forbade him to hold all three crowns.</p><p>On 6 October he abdicated the Neapolitan and Sicilian thrones in favour of Ferdinand, since his eldest son Philip had been excluded from the succession because of intellectual disability and his second son Charles was heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. A regency council was set up under the Tuscan Bernardo Tanucci, who neglected the boy's education and encouraged his love of pleasure and outdoor sports. Ferdinand founded the cadet House of Bourbon-Two Sicilies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ferdinand's minority ended in 1767, and his first act was the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. The following year he married Archduchess Maria Carolina, daughter of Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>, who used a clause of the marriage contract to gain influence in the council of state.</p><p>Tanucci was dismissed in 1777, and the Englishman Sir John Acton, appointed director of marine in 1779, became the leading minister and was largely responsible for reducing the administration to a system of espionage, corruption and cruelty. In 1798, at his wife's urging, Ferdinand went to war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 29 November, but the defeat of some columns sent him hurrying back. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 23 December 1798 on Nelson's ship HMS Vanguard, and his six-year-old son Prince Alberto died on the stormy voyage.</p><p>The French established the Parthenopean Republic in January 1799, and Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fabrizio-ruffo">Fabrizio Ruffo</a> recovered the mainland that May with British artillery, the Church and the pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> aristocracy. Several hundred French collaborators were then executed. After the French victory at Austerlitz Ferdinand fled again on 23 January 1806, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> proclaimed his brother Joseph king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Ferdinand kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under British protection, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-william-bentinck">Lord William Bentinck</a> insisted on constitutional reform and the queen was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> fell in 1815 Ferdinand returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, bound by a secret treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1816 he abolished the Sicilian constitution, against his oath, and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a> on 12 December. The revolt of General Guglielmo Pepe in July 1820 forced him to accept a constitution, but he repudiated it, and an Austrian army defeated the Neapolitans at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rieti">Rieti</a> on 7 March 1821 and occupied Naples. He suppressed the liberals and Carbonari and died in Naples on 4 January 1825.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles of Bourbon won the kingdom at the Battle of Bitonto in 1734, but the army of his son <b>Ferdinand IV</b> collapsed against the French in 1798.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ferdinand III of Sicily</b>, who as Ferdinand IV also ruled Naples, twice took refuge on the island during the French wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of the Two Sicilies was made in 1816, when the Bourbon <b>Ferdinand IV of Naples</b>, restored to his mainland throne by the Treaty of Casalanza and given back the island of Sicily, annulled the Sicilian constitution of 1812 that had virtually disempowered him there and merged…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>King Ferdinand IV</b> fled to Sicily under British protection, and Napoleon made his brother Joseph Bonaparte king of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-naples-1806-1815">Kingdom of Naples (1806–1815)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Kingdom of Naples attacked the French in Rome in late 1798 and was defeated, <b>King Ferdinand IV</b> fled to Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/state/parthenopean-republic">Parthenopean Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1799" aria-label="Naples on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 706 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.84,14.25&amp;year=1799">What was here in 1799? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
